1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,600
But how is this related to
the Prime Number Theorem?

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:06,950
Let's go back to the inexact form.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:08,900
There's an ln(n) here.

4
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:12,050
You surely know another series like this.

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,000
It's the harmonic series.

6
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:15,950
So what did we get?

7
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,500
So summing over all natural numbers

8
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:21,450
is like summing over the primes only,

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,800
but giving each prime p the weight ln(p).

10
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:28,150
It's just as if the primes around p

11
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:31,500
were spread ln(p) times more
sparsely than the integers,

12
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:34,450
and so they have to be
counted with more weight

13
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:36,800
to balance their contribution.

14
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:40,950
But this is exactly what the
Prime Number Theorem said.

15
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:45,300
Well, this doesn't really
prove the Prime Number Theorem,

16
00:00:45,450 --> 00:00:47,850
since it only tells us about averages.

17
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,400
There could be regions with
significantly fewer primes

18
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:54,750
and regions with significantly more.

19
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,900
But it gives us a sense of it.

20
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:01,050
In fact, one can deduce from
it the following weak version

21
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,600
of the Prime Number Theorem:

